import <wdoc.wcl>

concept templates {
  name     = "Templates"
  summary  = "The built-in templates webpage / book / presentation / ai_skill, their navigation blocks, and writing a custom template."
  related  = [sites, pages, skills, styling]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p <<PROSE
A **template** turns a site's pages into a particular shape of output. A site selects one with `default_template` (a page may override with `template`). Four are built in — `:webpage`, `:book`, `:presentation`, and `:ai_skill` — and each reads its own navigation block off the `site`.
PROSE

    h2 "webpage template"
    p <<PROSE
A Hugo-style site header, a sticky top navbar built from `menu`, and a reading column. Menu items use `page = <name>` for in-site links (validated against pages in this site) or `href = "…"` for external or cross-site URLs. Nested `item`s become dropdown groups.
PROSE
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
site marketing {
  default_template = :webpage
  title            = "My project"
  root             = true
  theme            = :nord
  menu {
    item "Home"     { page = index }
    item "Docs"     { href = "docs/" }
    item "More" {
      item "About"   { page = about }
      item "Contact" { page = contact }
    }
    item "Source"   { href = "https://github.com/example/proj" }
  }
}
SRC
    }

    h2 "book template"
    p <<PROSE
An mdBook-style fixed left sidebar with nested chapters and current-chapter highlight; reading column on the right. Chapters nest to any depth. A `chapter` with no `page =` is a grouping heading. A `chapter` pointing at an unknown page is a build error.
PROSE
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
site docs {
  default_template = :book
  title            = "Project Docs"
  theme            = :nord
  theme_toggle     = true
  toc {
    chapter "Intro"        { page = index }
    chapter "Guide" {
      chapter "Setup"      { page = setup }
      chapter "First run"  { page = first_run }
    }
  }
}
SRC
    }

    h2 "presentation template"
    p <<PROSE
A reveal.js-style slide deck: the whole site renders into a single `index.html`, navigated with the keyboard. The `deck` block lays out the 2-D grid — each `section` is a column, its `slide`s are rows — and each `slide` names a page that belongs to this site.
PROSE
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
site talk {
  default_template = :presentation
  title            = "My talk"
  theme            = :catppuccin
  deck {
    section "Intro" {
      slide title
      slide agenda
    }
    section "Main" {
      slide topic
    }
  }
}
SRC
    }
    p <<PROSE
Each `slide` must sit on its own line. Two in-slide blocks are deck-specific: `fragment { … }` is a step-reveal group (hidden until the presenter advances with Space), and `notes { … }` holds speaker notes (hidden in the deck, shown in the overlay toggled with **s**).
PROSE
    code "wcl" { source = <<'SRC'
page topic {
  h2 "Key points"
  fragment { p "Revealed on the first Space" }
  fragment { p "…then this one" }
  notes { p "Reminder: mention the benchmark numbers." }
}
SRC
    }

    h2 "ai_skill template"
    p <<PROSE
A fourth built-in: `default_template = :ai_skill` makes the site a Claude / agent skill folder, built by `wcl wdoc skill` (not `wcl wdoc build`). See the **skills** concept for the `skill { }` block and `file` blocks.
PROSE

    h2 "Custom templates"
    p <<PROSE
The built-ins are not special: a template is just a function from a `TemplateCtx` to a list of HTML fundamentals. Declare a `template <name> { render = fn(c: TemplateCtx) -> list<HtmlFundamental> … }` and select it with a site's `default_template` (e.g. `:blog`) or a page's `template` field. The stdlib exposes its chrome as composable parts (`wdoc_part_*`) plus one `wdoc_*_layout` per built-in, all resolved by bare name once you `import <wdoc.wcl>`.
PROSE
    callout "Parts resolve by bare name" { class = ["note"]  body = "Template parts are plain functions reached by name through `import <wdoc.wcl>` — don't define a `let` of your own named `wdoc_part_*` or `wdoc_*_layout`, or it will shadow the stdlib one." }

    h2 "Block reference"
    block_doc { type = Template  description = "A `template` block: a custom output shape — a `render` function from a `TemplateCtx` to a list of HTML fundamentals, selectable by `default_template`." }
    block_doc { type = Menu  description = "A `menu` block: the top navbar navigation for the `webpage` template, holding `item` entries." }
    block_doc { type = MenuItem  description = "An `item` in a `menu`: a label with a `page` (in-site link) or `href` (external / cross-site), nesting more `item`s as a dropdown group." }
    block_doc { type = Toc  description = "A `toc` block: the left-sidebar table of contents for the `book` template, holding nested `chapter` entries." }
    block_doc { type = Chapter  description = "A `chapter` in a `toc`: a label with an optional `page` (omit it for a grouping heading), nesting more chapters to any depth." }
    block_doc { type = Deck  description = "A `deck` block: the 2-D slide grid for the `presentation` template — `section`s are columns, their `slide`s are rows." }
    block_doc { type = DeckSectionBlock  description = "A `section` in a `deck`: one column of the slide grid, holding `slide` references." }
    block_doc { type = DeckSlideRef  description = "A `slide` in a deck `section`: a reference to a page that belongs to the presentation site." }
    block_doc { type = Fragment  description = "A `fragment` block: a step-reveal group inside a slide, hidden until the presenter advances." }
    block_doc { type = Notes  description = "A `notes` block: speaker notes inside a slide — hidden in the deck, shown in the presenter overlay." }
  }
}
